  "policy_id": "ref-09-cut-foreign-aid-by-50",
  "party": "Reform UK",
  "policy_title": "Cut foreign aid by 50%",
  "outcome": "O6",
  "verdict": {
    "outcome": "O6",
    "direction": "worsens",
    "magnitude": "minor",
    "time_horizon": "long-term",
    "confidence": "low",
    "plain": "Cutting foreign aid by half would reduce UK funding for international climate and environment programmes, potentially weakening global climate efforts. The evidence on the specifically environmental portion of the cut is sparse, so the magnitude is uncertain.",
    "biggest_unknown": "What share of the \u00a36 billion cut would fall on climate and environment programmes versus health, education, or humanitarian aid \u2014 and whether other donors would fill the gap.",
    "claims": [
      {
        "tier": "stated",
        "claim": "The policy cuts the foreign aid budget by 50%, saving \u00a36 billion, and includes a review of effectiveness including payments to global quangos.",
        "evidence_id": "M",
        "quote": "Reform UK will cut the foreign aid budget by 50%, saving \u00a36 billion, and undertake a major review of its effectiveness, including payments to global quangos."
      },
      {
        "tier": "projected",
        "claim": "Analysts say the cuts signal a withdrawal from UK efforts to tackle climate change.",
        "evidence_id": "E21",
        "quote": "This \"signals a withdrawal from efforts to tackle climate change, global poverty and inequality, and conflict and humanitarian needs\""
      },
      {
        "tier": "projected",
        "claim": "There are questions about whether specific pledges to maintain climate commitments can survive cuts of this scale.",
        "evidence_id": "E30",
        "quote": "former International Development Minister Anneliese Dodds resigned, stating it would be \"impossible to maintain these priorities\" given the scale of the cuts"
      }
    ],
    "rationale": "UK foreign aid funds international climate initiatives and environmental programmes in developing nations. A 50% cut would almost certainly reduce climate-related aid flows, weakening the UK's contribution to global emissions reduction, clean energy transitions in lower-income countries, and biodiversity programmes. The evidence signals that climate commitments are among those threatened (E21, E30), but provides no breakdown of how much of the cut falls specifically on climate and environment versus health, education, or humanitarian spending. The O6 harm is therefore real in direction \u2014 less UK climate finance abroad, weaker influence on global emissions trajectories \u2014 but hard to quantify from the evidence provided. The effect is long-term because climate and biodiversity impacts accumulate over decades. Magnitude is assessed as minor given that UK aid is a small share of global climate finance and the evidence does not demonstrate a measurable shift in global emissions or biodiversity outcomes attributable to UK aid at this scale. The confidence is low because the evidence on O6-specific channels is sparse and mostly advocacy-sourced.",
    "time_split": {
      "near_direction": "negligible",
      "long_direction": "worsens",
      "note": "Immediate budget cut has negligible near-term environmental effect; long-term climate finance shortfalls and reduced UK influence on global emissions trajectory accumulate over decades."
    },
